[decoded] "During the day, sunlight travels through the atmosphere in all directions. As it passes through the air, it scatters shorter wavelengths of light, specifically blue and violet, more intensely than longer wavelengths like red. Although violet scatters even more, our eyes are less sensitive to violet, so we perceive the sky as blue.\n\nAt sunset, the sun is lower on the horizon, causing sunlight to travel through a much thicker layer of the atmosphere. This increased path length scatters almost all blue light away from our line of sight. The remaining light is dominated by longer wavelengths, which appear red, orange, or pink. Essentially, the thicker atmosphere filters out the blue light, leaving the warm colors to reach our eyes."
